Self-tanners are cosmetic products that temporarily darken the skin without requiring exposure to sunlight or artificial UV radiation. Common formats include lotions, sprays, mousses, and gels, with formulations often using dihydroxyacetone (DHA) or erythrulose to produce a tanning effect through interaction with amino acids in the skin's outer layer.

The market is evolving as consumers seek alternatives to conventional sunbathing and tanning beds. Manufacturers are expanding product portfolios to offer more natural-looking results, easier application, improved skin hydration, and formulations designed for different skin tones and preferences.

North America was the largest regional market in 2025, while Asia-Pacific represents an important growth opportunity as beauty retail, e-commerce, and consumer awareness of sunless tanning continue to develop.

